; Two focused aggregates beyond `scans` (gh-ocannl-783). Same arrangement and same completeness
; check, a different question: `scans` collects the tests that read the REPOSITORY, these collect
; the tests that answer for one SUBSYSTEM, so that a change confined to it is revalidated without a
; backend-wide directory run. Each family alias is spelled the same in every directory that has
; members, which is what makes `dune build @metal-codegen` from the root run the whole family
; wherever it lives -- `arrayjit/test/dune` carries the other half of both of these.
;
; Membership is DERIVED, never listed twice: `test/operations/env_var_deps` asks each stanza what it
; IS -- a stanza whose backend marker names `metal` is a Metal member, a stanza whose modules read
; the resource-lifecycle instrumentation is a lifecycle member -- and fails on one this list omits,
; the same way it derives the `scans` family from the rules that glob the repository. A list checked
; against a second copy of itself could only ever confirm that the copy still says what it says.
;
; The members are `(test)` stanzas, so what these list are the per-test aliases dune generates from
; 3.20 rather than hand-written rule fields -- which is the reason `dune-project` declares 3.20 as
; the floor rather than 3.18.

; The Metal-pinned tests: the executed Metal-only guards and the emitted-MSL structural ones. Every
; stanza here names `metal` in its backend marker, so no other box can judge any of them, and the
; backend-wide `@test/operations/runtest` that would otherwise be the way to reach them carries
; several hundred legs that have nothing to do with Metal.

(alias
 (name metal-codegen)
 (deps
  (alias runtest-env_spelling_gate)
  (alias runtest-test_metal_pool_bindings)
  (alias runtest-test_metal_guarded_gather_codegen)
  (alias runtest-test_metal_pool_slot_width)))

; The resource-lifecycle probes: the tests that drive `Ir.Resource_fault_injection` or read
