; gh-ocannl-655: the provenance guarantees of Test_utils.Generated, which ~30 tests' structural
; assertions now rest on. Every one of those guarantees fires only when something has already gone
; wrong, so none of them is exercised by the tests that depend on them -- the code that decides a
; kernel is NOT this run's runs, in a green suite, never. Hence a probe whose modes stage each
; failure deliberately.
;
; The assertion is the EXIT STATUS, not the output: Verdict failures exit 1 from a shared teardown,
; so `(with-accepted-exit-codes 1 ...)` is exactly "this must be refused", and a mode that starts
; passing fails the build rather than printing a promotable line. `ignore-exit-codes` would not do:
; a run that stops for some other reason has to fail here too.
;
; One rule, one progn: every mode shares this executable's artifact directory, so running them as
; separate rules would let dune schedule them concurrently and have one mode's init sweep away
; another's staged artifact. A single action is sequential by construction. The two explicit-prefix
; modes take a prefix that is NOT this executable's name, which is what puts Generated on its
; shared-directory path (no sweep, arming required); the flat mode takes the legacy `.`.

(executable
 (name generated_provenance)
 (modules generated_provenance)
 (libraries base stdio unix arrayjit.utils arrayjit.verdict test_utils))

(rule
 ; ocannl-backend: none -- links arrayjit.utils, test_utils and unix, and writes its artifacts by
 ; hand rather than compiling any. Its `backend_name = "cc"` is the label a generated file's name
 ; carries, not a backend it selects: what is under test is how Generated decides a file on disk
 ; belongs to this run.
 ; One alias, not two (gh-ocannl-726): this rule used to name `runtest` and
 ; `generated_provenance` both, which made `dune build @test/operations/generated_provenance` build
 ; the whole directory -- a rule on two aliases makes building either build both. The per-test
 ; alias below is aggregated back onto `runtest` by the stanza near the top of this file.
 (alias runtest-generated_provenance)
 (package neural_nets_lib)
 (deps
  (alias runtest-env_spelling_gate)
  ocannl_config
  (env_var OCANNL_BUILD_FILES_PREFIX)
  %{dep:generated_provenance.exe})
 (action
  (setenv
   OCANNL_BUILD_FILES_PREFIX
   ""
   (progn
    (run ./generated_provenance.exe symlink)
    (run ./generated_provenance.exe fresh)
    (run ./generated_provenance.exe armed_overwrite)
    ; The modes that must be refused are not run from here (gh-ocannl-692). A refusal announces
    ; itself with this repository's failure marker -- `FAIL:` and `FAILED:` -- so running one with
    ; the suite's streams inherited put those words into the log of a green run and cost `grep FAIL`
    ; its meaning. `refusals` runs each of them as a child whose output it captures, and requires
    ; both the exit status `with-accepted-exit-codes` used to state and the specific refusal
    ; message, which no exit status can tell apart from any other way of failing.
    (run ./generated_provenance.exe refusals)))))

; The non-emptiness guarantee of the quantified claims (gh-ocannl-729). Same construction as
